From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] query_cache: hold the occlusion query at its bound instead of resetting it

When a query's slot count passes the bound in CloseCounter, ResetCounter releases the query and,
through a SyncOperation, sets amend_value and accumulation_value to zero. The guest then reads the
occlusion report as 0 and takes that as "everything is occluded": it skips the whole 3D scene and
presents the clear colour, which is the white screen with the HUD still drawn and the audio still
playing.

The bound was one bank, 256 slots, and patch 0413 raised it to 32 banks. A PS5 console log has a
145 s session crossing that once:

    86.445846 CloseCounter: Query slot count past 32 banks (8201 slots): dropping it as a reset
             would (1th drop)

8,201 slots is past 8,192, so that bound reset a live count. Ocarina's own title screen and file
select reach 16,384 slots over 64 banks, so 64 banks is a window this game is known to fit in, and
8,201 clears it with 2x to spare. This raises the bound to 64 banks, which is 128 KiB of query
results.

Upstream's history for this number, from the PR patches themselves: zortos293/Lichii #558 raised it
to BANK_SIZE * 64, #564 argued it back down to * 16 and #566 to * 32, and #570 (closed, not merged)
proposed * 32. This takes #558's 64, for the headroom the PS5 measurement needs. It is not a claim
that upstream settled there.

At the bound the query is now held rather than reset. Three things are true at once, and the hold is
what satisfies all three:

- A reset lies. It abandons the query and zeroes the carried count, which is the symptom.
- Pausing alone is not a bound. PauseCounter only records EndQuery; the next StartCounter still calls
  ReserveHostQuery on the same query, so a window that never closes keeps allocating. That growth is
  what Lichii #121 records hanging an AMD host at a game's save select with the split on, and the PS5
  runs that split. Raising the bound without holding it would move that hang, not remove it.
- Releasing the query's banks while preserving the accumulated result needs the segmented accumulator
  that ReplicateCurrentQueryIfNeeded does only for a pending sync - a change to the query's lifetime
  that has not been exercised here.

So StartCounter returns early while the hold is set, CloseCounter logs the moment it is taken, and the
guest's own ResetCounter clears it. The count the guest reads stays, and no further host query slots
are allocated.

The submission boundary keeps its behaviour and only loses a misleading comment. EndPendingOperations
already pauses rather than resets for Ocarina, through ZpassCut::PauseNow, which is Lichii #121 by
Kajcsi - Fritchii lists it for Ocarina of Time as "the occlusion counter is paused, not reset, at
each submission". The comment above it claimed the opposite - "Off by default: with the split on,
pausing hung an AMD host at a game's save select" - and read as the reason the pause was not in, when
it is. That hang is why the pause stays per-title rather than becoming unconditional.

What this can break. Samples taken after the hold are not counted, so the window undercounts until the
guest's counter reset: that is the deliberate trade against a reset, which lies about the whole window
instead. If a guest never resets its counter the hold stays for the session and Ocarina's visibility
stops updating, which is what the log line is for. Taking the hold inside a guest's open window also
changes ZpassCut's cut-window bookkeeping the way a reset would have, only without the report going to
0; EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_CUT_LOG=1 shows it.

Measured, not a guess: the drop is in the console log quoted above, and the 16,384 slots the title
screen reaches is from the port's own record of it. The effect of the change is not yet
console-tested. The confirming run is one PS5 session with EDEN_NX2_SPLIT=2, EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_PAUSE=1,
EDEN_NX2_QUERY_KEEP_PENDING=1 and EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_CUT_LOG=1, expecting no query above 64 banks and no
report of 0 after a cut.

diff --git a/src/video_core/renderer_vulkan/vk_query_cache.cpp b/src/video_core/renderer_vulkan/vk_query_cache.cpp
index 01ade09b4c..d9d4a0874a 100644
--- a/src/video_core/renderer_vulkan/vk_query_cache.cpp
+++ b/src/video_core/renderer_vulkan/vk_query_cache.cpp
@@ -169,6 +169,11 @@ public:
 
     void StartCounter() override {
         samples_at_stake = true;
+        // The bound in CloseCounter latched this streamer: the retained query is not started again
+        // until the guest resets its counter, so no further host query slots are allocated.
+        if (paused_at_bound) {
+            return;
+        }
         if (has_started) {
             return;
         }
@@ -198,6 +203,9 @@ public:
     }
 
     void ResetCounter() override {
+        // The guest reset its counter: the window it was counting is over, so the hold the bound in
+        // CloseCounter put on this streamer is released and a new window starts.
+        paused_at_bound = false;
         if (reset_by_submission) {
             reset_by_submission = false;
         } else {
@@ -230,32 +238,41 @@ public:
         samples_at_stake = false;
     }
 
-    // Called at each submission with EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_PAUSE=1. The running query carries the count
-    // across it: a guest's reset-to-report window can span one.
+    // Called at each submission. The running query carries the count across it: a guest's
+    // reset-to-report window can span one.
     void CloseCounter() override {
         PauseCounter();
         // A title that counts without resetting or reporting grows the query by a slot at every
-        // submission and render pass, and the query keeps every bank it touches alive. Ocarina of
-        // Time's title screen and file select reached 16,384 slots over 64 banks.
+        // submission and render pass, and it keeps every bank it touches alive until the query is
+        // released (BankPool reuses a bank only once it is closed and unreferenced).
         //
-        // One bank is too low for gameplay. Lichii raised this same bound four times for this same
-        // symptom - zortos293/Lichii #558 to BANK_SIZE * 64, and #564/#566/#570 for the reason in
-        // their comment: "Gameplay near water and windows can exceed a single 256-slot bank, so the
-        // limit must not trigger there." A reset here abandons the running query (ResetCounter ->
-        // AbandonCurrentQuery), so the count it held is lost and the report the water surface
-        // switches on flips - the black, flickering water. 32 banks (8,192 slots) is far past any
-        // gameplay window and well under the 16,384 the title screen already reaches, so growth
-        // stays bounded: 32 banks is 64 KiB of query results, against 128 KiB for the title screen.
-        // Logged at the 1st, 2nd, 4th, 8th... drop, so a long window during play cannot go unnoticed.
-        if (current_query && current_query->size_slots > SamplesQueryBank::BANK_SIZE * 32) {
-            static u64 bank_bound_resets{};
-            if ((++bank_bound_resets & (bank_bound_resets - 1)) == 0) {
-                LOG_INFO(Render_Vulkan,
-                         "Query slot count past 32 banks ({} slots): dropping it as a reset would "
-                         "({}th drop)",
-                         current_query->size_slots, bank_bound_resets);
-            }
-            ResetCounter();
+        // One bank (256 slots) is far too low. A PS5 console log has one 145 s session crossing it
+        // once - "Query slot count past 32 banks (8201 slots): dropping it as a reset would (1th
+        // drop)" at 86.4 s - and that reset threw away a live count, which is the white screen with
+        // the HUD still drawn. Ocarina's own title screen and file select reach 16,384 slots over 64
+        // banks, so 64 banks is a window this game is known to fit in, and 8,201 clears it with 2x
+        // to spare. 64 banks is 128 KiB of query results.
+        //
+        // Upstream's history for this bound: zortos293/Lichii #558 raised it to BANK_SIZE * 64,
+        // #564 argued it back down to * 16 and #566 to * 32, and #570 (closed, unmerged) proposed
+        // * 32. This takes #558's 64, for the headroom the PS5 log needs.
+        //
+        // Hold the query here; do not reset it. ResetCounter calls AbandonCurrentQuery and, through
+        // a SyncOperation, zeroes amend_value and accumulation_value, so the guest reads the report
+        // as 0 and culls the whole scene. Pausing without holding is not a bound either: the next
+        // StartCounter still calls ReserveHostQuery and grows this same query, so a window that
+        // never closes would allocate without limit - which is the query growth Lichii #121 records
+        // hanging an AMD host at a game's save select. Holding does both: the count the guest reads
+        // stays, and no further host query slots are allocated until the guest's own counter reset
+        // releases the hold. The cost is that samples after the hold are not counted, so the window
+        // undercounts until that reset, where a reset would lie about all of it.
+        if (!paused_at_bound && current_query &&
+            current_query->size_slots >= SamplesQueryBank::BANK_SIZE * 64) {
+            paused_at_bound = true;
+            LOG_INFO(Render_Vulkan,
+                     "Query slot count reached 64 banks ({} slots): holding the count until the "
+                     "guest resets its counter, not a reset",
+                     current_query->size_slots);
         }
     }
 
@@ -700,6 +717,9 @@ private:
     bool window_open{};
     u32 cut_note{};
     bool reset_by_submission{};
+    /// Set when the slot bound in CloseCounter is reached: the retained query is held, and no further
+    /// host query slots are allocated, until the guest's own counter reset clears it.
+    bool paused_at_bound{};
 };
 
 // Transform feedback queries
